---
import DocPage from "../layouts/DocPage.astro";

const title = "sf-intelligence FAQ: Salesforce MCP, Privacy, Setup & Licensing";
const description =
  "Answers about setup, offline operation, read-only access, supported Salesforce metadata, MCP clients, licensing, limits and troubleshooting.";

const jsonld = [
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Does sf-intelligence write to or change my Salesforce org?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "No. It is read-only and has no write path. The first refresh runs sf project retrieve, which only reads metadata. No tool deploys, edits, or deletes anything in your org.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Does it send my org data to the cloud or to an AI provider?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "Vault answers make no network calls. The metadata index lives on your machine in an org-kb/ directory, and the MCP server reads those local files to answer. The only time it talks to Salesforce is during a refresh (to retrieve metadata) or when you explicitly enable the opt-in, read-only live plane for record counts and samples.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "How is this different from just asking ChatGPT or Claude about Salesforce?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "A general model answers from generic Salesforce training data, which is often wrong for your specific org. sf-intelligence answers only from the metadata you actually retrieved, cites every component with its canonical ID, and asks a clarifying question when a name is ambiguous instead of guessing.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What is the difference between the offline and live planes?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "The offline plane answers from your last refresh's local vault - schema, dependencies, permissions, source - and is the default. The live plane runs capped, read-only SOQL against the connected org for record-level facts like counts and samples, and is off until you grant consent once per org. Hybrid answers fuse both and disclose each provenance.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Can it tell me how many records are in an object?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "Only with the live plane enabled. The offline vault stores schema and source, not rows, so record-level questions need a read-only live query (sfi.live_count) or a direct sf data query against the org.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "How much does sf-intelligence cost?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "It is free and source-available under the MIT + Commons Clause license, distributed on npm as sf-intelligence.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What do I need to run it?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "Node.js 20 or newer and an authenticated Salesforce CLI (sf) pointed at the org you want to index. It runs in Claude Code, Claude Desktop, or any MCP client.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Are its answers always correct?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "Answers are grounded in real metadata, but each is tagged so you know how much to trust it. Confidence is declared, parsed, or heuristic; heuristic findings from the regex Apex scanner can have false positives and should be spot-checked. It also performs static analysis, so dynamic SOQL and reflective Apex are invisible - a 'no references found' result means 'no static evidence', not 'definitely unused'.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "How do I keep the answers up to date?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "Run sfi refresh (or /sfi-refresh in Claude Code) after you deploy changes to the org. sfi.health_check reports whether the vault is healthy, degraded, or unhealthy and tells you when a rebuild is due.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<DocPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/faq"
  heading="Frequently asked questions about sf-intelligence"
  intro="The questions people ask before they point sf-intelligence at a production Salesforce org."
  crumbs={[{ label: "faq", href: "/faq" }]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "FAQ", path: "/faq" }]}
  jsonld={jsonld}
>
  <section>
    <div class="container" style="max-width:820px;">

      <details class="faq-item" id="write" open>
        <summary>Does it write to or change my Salesforce org?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p><strong>No.</strong> It is read-only and has no write path. The first refresh runs <code>sf project retrieve</code>, which only reads metadata. No tool deploys, edits, or deletes anything in your org.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item" id="privacy">
        <summary>Does it send my org data to the cloud or to an AI provider?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>Vault answers make <strong>no network calls</strong>. The metadata index lives on your machine in an <code>org-kb/</code> directory, and the MCP server reads those local files to answer. The only times it talks to Salesforce are during a refresh (to retrieve metadata) or when you explicitly enable the opt-in, read-only live plane for record counts and samples. What you index stays yours.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item" id="vs-chatbot">
        <summary>How is this different from asking ChatGPT or Claude about Salesforce?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>A general model answers from generic Salesforce training data, which is often wrong for <em>your</em> org. sf-intelligence answers only from the metadata you actually retrieved, cites every component with its canonical ID (like <code>CustomField:Account.Industry__c</code>), and asks a clarifying question when a name is ambiguous instead of guessing.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item" id="planes">
        <summary>What is the difference between the offline and live planes?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>The <strong>offline</strong> plane answers from your last refresh's local vault - schema, dependencies, permissions, source - and is the default. The <strong>live</strong> plane runs capped, read-only SOQL against the connected org for record-level facts like counts and samples, and is off until you grant consent once per org. <strong>Hybrid</strong> answers fuse both and disclose each provenance. Live answers never silently backfill a stale vault claim.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item" id="records">
        <summary>Can it tell me how many records are in an object?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>Only with the live plane enabled. The offline vault stores schema and source, not rows, so record-level questions need a read-only live query (<code>sfi.live_count</code>) or a direct <code>sf data query</code> against the org.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item" id="cost">
        <summary>How much does it cost?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>Free and source-available under the <a href="/licensing">MIT + Commons Clause</a> license, distributed on npm as <code>sf-intelligence</code>.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item" id="requirements">
        <summary>What do I need to run it?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p><a href="https://nodejs.org" rel="noopener">Node.js 20+</a> and an authenticated <a href="https://developer.salesforce.com/tools/salesforcecli" rel="noopener">Salesforce CLI</a> (<code>sf</code>) pointed at the org you want to index. It runs in Claude Code, Claude Desktop, or any MCP client. See the <a href="/getting-started">getting-started guide</a>.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item" id="accuracy">
        <summary>Are its answers always correct?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>Answers are grounded in real metadata, but each is tagged so you know how much to trust it. Confidence is <code>declared</code>, <code>parsed</code>, or <code>heuristic</code> - heuristic findings from the regex Apex scanner can have false positives and should be spot-checked. It also performs static analysis, so dynamic SOQL and reflective Apex are invisible: a “no references found” result means “no static evidence”, not “definitely unused”. See the <a href="/trust">security and evidence model</a> for how every answer is labelled and tested.</p></div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item" id="fresh">
        <summary>How do I keep the answers up to date?</summary>
        <div class="answer"><p>Run <code>sfi refresh</code> (or <code>/sfi-refresh</code> in Claude Code) after you deploy changes. <code>sfi.health_check</code> reports whether the vault is <code>healthy</code>, <code>degraded</code>, or <code>unhealthy</code> and tells you when a rebuild is due.</p></div>
      </details>

      <div style="padding-top:42px;">
        <div class="cta-box">
          <h2 class="screen-type">Still have a question?</h2>
          <p>The getting-started guide covers setup end to end, and the capability map shows everything it can answer.</p>
          <div class="cta-actions">
            <a class="btn btn-solid" href="/getting-started">getting started</a>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/capabilities">capabilities</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocPage>
